; 13-check-that-a-bad-proof-is-rejected.kurt
;
; Check that a bad proof is rejected
;
; Use `expect "KIND"` for an example or regression that should fail. It checks the category of the
; error; a second string, `expect "ProofError" "can not derive"`, is a text that the message must
; contain.

bool A, B
use A

expect "ProofError"
    B

true

; The kinds: `ScanError` (a symbol can't be scanned), `ParseError` (the line can't be read),
; `EvalError` (readable, but not allowed -- e.g. wrong arguments of a keyword), `TypeError`
; (boolean or not, sorts), `ProofError` (a claim can't be derived, a block can't close). If
; the block succeeds instead of raising the named error, the expectation itself fails.
;
; Use this pattern in teaching material and most negative regression proofs. Give the text only
; when the message itself is the behavior under test:

expect "ProofError" "can not derive `B`"
    B
